**What**

`gix_store::store` and `store_anonymous` both take `message: Option<&str>`.
`gix-forge` passes `None` at every call site, so every commit in the forge
history reads `store issue/<id>`. No reason, no what-changed, no why. There is
no `-m` flag anywhere in the CLI.

Separately, `issue log` prints full object ids and ISO timestamps and nothing
else -- no author, no summary, no diff. Raw `git log -p` on the same ref is
dramatically more informative.

**Where**

- `crates/gix-forge/src/lib.rs:109` -- `store.store(KIND, &self.id, &value, None)`
- `crates/gix-forge/src/lib.rs:118`, `:232`, `:329`, `:338` -- same `None`
- `crates/git-forge/src/main.rs:1168` -- `print_log`

**Evidence**

What `issue log` gives you:

    issue history 4cd8865f:
      - 75ac16491fea7180442ef08774b723e68081bf1b  2026-07-28 10:34:02 +0900
      - 64a25ccbdf989805f7598d96e42e3a1295f91703  2026-07-28 10:34:02 +0900
      - 4cd8865fc977ec51cddb5ee1cad820a9fed244d8  2026-07-28 10:32:24 +0900

What `git log -p` on the identical ref gives you:

    commit 75ac164 store issue/4cd8865f
    --- a/status
    +++ b/status
    -open
    +closed
    commit 64a25cc store issue/4cd8865f
    --- a/title
    +++ b/title
    -First issue
    +First issue (edited)

**Why it matters**

The second block is the best thing in this repository. One field per blob means
an ordinary `git diff` produces a perfect field-level audit trail of an issue,
for free, with no forge-specific tooling. That is the argument for the whole
project, rendered by a command the audience already trusts. Being able to say
here is the issue history, and I am reading it with plain `git`, no server, no
API is worth more than any bespoke output could be.

And the tool`s own `log` command throws it away. It shows the two least useful
columns and omits the one that matters. A newcomer will use `issue log`,
conclude the history is opaque, and never discover that `git log -p` works.

The discarded commit message compounds it. Git`s central affordance for
recording intent is available on every write and used on none, so even the good
view cannot answer why an issue was closed. GitHub cannot do this at all; you
can, and you are not.

**Options**

- Option A -- Plumb `-m/--message` through `new` and `edit`, default to a
  generated summary naming the changed fields (`close issue`,
  `retitle issue`). Trade-off: none, the parameter already exists and is being
  passed `None`.
- Option B -- Make `issue log` show author, relative date, summary, and a
  `--patch` flag rendering the per-field diff. Trade-off: reimplements a slice
  of `git log`, which is arguably not your job.
- Option C -- Make `issue log` a thin wrapper that shells out to or reuses the
  `git log` machinery on `refs/forge/issue/<id>`, so it is exactly as good as
  `git log` by construction and stays that way. Trade-off: less control over
  presentation.

**Recommendation**

A and C. High confidence on A -- it is a parameter already threaded to the
right place and currently nulled out. C over B because the point being made is
that this is just Git, and the most persuasive way to make that point is for
the forge`s history command to visibly be Git. Also worth putting `git log -p
refs/forge/issue/<id>` on a slide verbatim, whatever else you do.